{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1211432-marvel-vs-capcom-vs-white-wolf-a-homebrew-wod-setting","id":1211432,"title":"Marvel vs. Capcom vs. White Wolf (A Homebrew WoD Setting)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-04-25T08:38:52-07:00","id":1211432,"title":"Marvel vs. Capcom vs. White Wolf (A Homebrew WoD Setting)","content":"<div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KKfwOyePAJ4\" data-code=\"KKfwOyePAJ4\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/KKfwOyePAJ4/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> Alright, I sort of came up with this idea this morning and I figured I'd share it with you guys. It's sort of a fun crossover setting that is a love letter to the era of my childhood days. That era being the 1990's and very early 2000's, of course. So I decided to make a fun and heroic crossover setting for World of Darkness, even if it's a little unconventional in nature.<br /> <br /> The crossover is specifically set during an unspecified point in the 1990's and invokes the feel and continuity of the Marvel cartoons that aired on Fox Kids back in the 90's, most notably X-Men and Spider-Man. In terms of the White Wolf side of things, it is done in the style of First Edition and invokes that sort of atmosphere and aesthetics and does not recognize the metaplot in any way, shape, or form. So Gangrel are still in the Camarilla, the Ravnos are a full clan, and there was no Week of Nightmares or Avatar Storm, etc.<br /> <br /> The core elements of the setting are listed below.<br /> <br /> <b>White Wolf </b><br /> <br /> <i>Vampire: The Masquerade, </i>First Edition<br /> <i>Werewolf: The Apocalypse, </i>First Edition<br /> <i>Mage: The Ascension, </i>First Edition<br /> <br /> <b>Capcom</b><br /> <br /> <i>Resident Evil<br /> Street Fighter<br /> Final Fight</i><br /> <br /> <b>Marvel</b><br /> <br /> <i>X-Men</i> (1992 Animated Series)<br /> <i>Spider-Man</i> (1994 Animated Series)<br /> <br /> <b>Other</b><br /> <br /> <i>Sailor Moon </i>(Cloverway Dub)<br /> <br /> <i>Changeling: The Dreaming </i>and <i>Wraith: The Oblivion </i>are only loosely canon in a &quot;Broad Strokes&quot; sort of way, while <i>Kindred of the East, Hunter: The Reckoning, Mummy: The Resurrection, </i>and especially <i>Demon: The Fallen </i>are NOT canon in this crossover setting.<br /> <br /> The location of the crossover is New York (and parts of New Jersey), with the bulk of the action taking place in New York City and the surrounding metropolitan areas (although Raccoon City is located in rural Upstate New York) and it must be stated that in this setting, the Sabbat never controlled NYC. Ever. Instead, New York City is a Camarilla city with some Anarchs as well.<br /> <br /> I may take a lot of the NPC's from the original Chicago by Night and port them over to New York City instead, with some changes for setting such as Al Capone being replaced with Carlo Gambino and Modius being Prince of Newark, New Jersey instead of Gary, Indiana.<br /> <br /> Overall, this crossover setting is a love letter to the play-style commonly derided as &quot;Vampions&quot; or &quot;Supers With Fangs&quot; and it throws out the premise of Personal Horror entirely as I personally don't like Personal Horror as a theme. For this specific crossover setting, I say let's put the Goth and Punk stuff aside for the time being and instead bring on the trenchcoats and katanas!<br /> <br /> However, it won't just be mindless combat. There will be a major political component to gameplay as well, especially for Vampire and Mage. It is a game that can support many different themes (even Personal Horror if you want it to) and I want to run it as either an online sandbox campaign or as a shared fan fiction universe.<br /> <br /> So, what do you all think of this nostalgia-fueled and action-packed setting of dark modern adventure? And have you ever considered doing your own World of Darkness crossover settings? I know this setting is very different from the standard White Wolf fare but I think it could be very fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 04-25-2018, 11:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T12:07:56-07:00","id":1211496,"title":"","content":"I think that you're going to be in trouble with the clunky mechanics of first edition, particularly with Werewolf and Mage, the former of which had grossly inflated Renown totals, the latter being a game of magick that didn't have functional magick rules until the Player's Guide was released. Also, as a reminder, Dodge added to soak instead of negating attack successes, and there were variable soak difficulties by weapon (Impact).<br /> <br /> But hey, at least no Thrall of the Wyrm, I guess. Mage and Werewolf still can't coexist in the setting with both being true, though, so that's going to be a big headache looming on the horizon for you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T13:03:59-07:00","id":1211520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1211432-marvel-vs-capcom-vs-white-wolf-a-homebrew-wod-setting?p=1211496#post1211496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that you're going to be in trouble with the clunky mechanics of first edition, particularly with Werewolf and Mage, the former of which had grossly inflated Renown totals, the latter being a game of magick that didn't have functional magick rules until the Player's Guide was released. Also, as a reminder, Dodge added to soak instead of negating attack successes, and there were variable soak difficulties by weapon (Impact).<br /> <br /> But hey, at least no Thrall of the Wyrm, I guess. Mage and Werewolf still can't coexist in the setting with both being true, though, so that's going to be a big headache looming on the horizon for you.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Meh, I play fast and loose with the canon and cosmology anyway so Mage and Werewolf co-existing doesn't really bother me that much.<br /> <br /> I might change over to Second Edition mechanics for mechanical simplicity's sake. I would go 20th Anniversary Edition, but they dropped the ball big time on Mage 20, which is sad because Vampire 20 and Werewolf 20 were awesome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-26T12:14:42-07:00","id":1211926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1211432-marvel-vs-capcom-vs-white-wolf-a-homebrew-wod-setting?p=1211520#post1211520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Meh, I play fast and loose with the canon and cosmology anyway so Mage and Werewolf co-existing doesn't really bother me that much.</div> </div> </div> </div>This isn't really a thing that'll never conceivably come up. For example, Mage says that outer space is part of the Umbra, Werewolf assumes that it, and by extension the rest of the universe, wherever it may fall, is physical. It also doesn't leave much variability in what the Deep Umbra is, so different people are going to be going to different places when they're trying to go to the same place. Just a heads up for that specific case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I might change over to Second Edition mechanics for mechanical simplicity's sake. I would go 20th Anniversary Edition, but they dropped the ball big time on Mage 20, which is sad because Vampire 20 and Werewolf 20 were awesome. </div> </div> </div>I must admit that M20 seems like a headache because of its &quot;mandatory optional&quot; list of secondary abilities and the shuffling around of various Ability concepts and which category they fit under, but those can be remedied by using the other anniversary edition's rules.<br /> <br /> (Also, second edition setting material for Mage and Werewolf has an even more confusing divergence on cosmology, as what Mage 2nd called the High Umbra was part of Werewolf 2nd's Deep Umbra.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 04-26-2018, 03:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}